VALUES ('e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 'ccs-practice-3', 'CCS Practice Paper 3', 'ccs', ARRAY['ICD-10-CM', 'ICD-10-PCS', 'HCC Risk Adjustment', 'Two-Midnight Rule', 'Oncology Coding', 'Cardiology Coding', 'HL7/FHIR', 'Provider Queries', 'Regulatory Compliance']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 3, 240);

-- ── Section: Domain 1 — Coding Knowledge and Skills (40 questions) — Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank. ──

VALUES ('4fc4ea47-6205-4e8e-9ac9-d7ab6b2f5dc1', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 1, 'A patient with a documented history of breast cancer, status post lumpectomy and completed chemotherapy two years ago, presents for a routine surveillance mammogram with no current evidence of disease. The appropriate primary code for this encounter is most likely:');
VALUES ('4fc4ea47-6205-4e8e-9ac9-d7ab6b2f5dc1', '5943407a-ca42-4f85-ac39-a74c49b05380', 'Once treatment is completed and there is no evidence of remaining disease, a personal history of malignancy Z code is used rather than an active neoplasm code — a core distinction in the neoplasm coding guidelines between active disease and history of a resolved malignancy.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5c65e57f-d3c0-485d-8474-adb043106558', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 2, 'A patient undergoes radiation therapy for an active malignancy, with the encounter solely for radiation treatment. Per the neoplasm coding guideline for encounters limited to radiotherapy, the correct sequencing is:');
VALUES ('5c65e57f-d3c0-485d-8474-adb043106558', 'b1524fd4-f913-4c0f-8af5-c476190029fd', 'Mirroring the chemotherapy-encounter guideline, when an encounter is solely for radiotherapy, the encounter Z code is sequenced first with the malignancy as an additional diagnosis — a frequently tested parallel structure to the chemotherapy sequencing rule.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5b9d0d16-2188-4485-853d-ccb5ff935726', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 3, 'A patient is admitted with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) and undergoes emergency PCI with stent placement in the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies to the stent placement itself?');
VALUES ('5b9d0d16-2188-4485-853d-ccb5ff935726', '1c126c12-00e8-4d9b-805c-b64c218eeddb', 'Stent placement to open/expand a narrowed or occluded vessel is coded as Dilation with a device value reflecting the stent left in place — a commonly tested cardiology ICD-10-PCS scenario, distinct from Restriction (narrowing) or Occlusion (closing).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('141544ab-6328-442e-86f8-58a5ab8e6faf', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 4, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is used for a coronary artery bypass graft (CABG), reflecting the creation of a new pathway to route blood around a blocked vessel?');
VALUES ('141544ab-6328-442e-86f8-58a5ab8e6faf', 'c791ad8a-a0e9-4126-88a9-c46162ab27e7', 'Bypass is specifically defined as altering the route of passage of the contents of a tubular body part — CABG''s defining clinical purpose (rerouting blood flow around a blockage) maps directly to this root operation, distinct from Dilation (which widens the existing lumen rather than creating an alternate route).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b462b75a-8e88-4b05-b655-825fc8d5c8d4', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 5, 'A patient''s documentation states ''NSTEMI'' (non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction). Per ICD-10-CM guidance on MI type/timing, which of the following is most relevant to correct code assignment?');
VALUES ('b462b75a-8e88-4b05-b655-825fc8d5c8d4', '8a27bfeb-5403-4f66-90ca-ee9f742799e1', 'ICD-10-CM distinguishes STEMI from NSTEMI with distinct codes (and further specifies coronary artery/wall site for STEMI when documented) — conflating the two, or defaulting to unspecified when the type is clearly documented, under-codes the specificity actually supported.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('90bf58dd-f5ba-41bd-9a88-d23924a73c0d', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 6, 'A patient with an acute MI documented as occurring within the 4-week (28-day) timeframe of the initial MI episode, representing a new infarction in a different coronary artery territory, would generally be coded using:');
VALUES ('90bf58dd-f5ba-41bd-9a88-d23924a73c0d', '83c0524e-f892-4a89-b53a-584ea998125e', 'ICD-10-CM has a specific category for a subsequent (new) AMI occurring within 4 weeks of a prior one — a coding nuance distinct from simply re-using the original AMI code or coding it merely as a history.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('33466491-c54c-4d08-9e14-0eacac7f20e0', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 7, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation would most likely apply to a total hip arthroplasty (complete replacement of the hip joint with a prosthetic implant)?');
VALUES ('33466491-c54c-4d08-9e14-0eacac7f20e0', '0b1b9a05-dce3-44b3-b8e6-361cb81b8a74', 'Total joint arthroplasty is a classic Replacement scenario — the natural joint (or the diseased portion) is physically taken out and replaced with a prosthetic device that takes its place and function.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('170f4cb1-e75a-42f9-9255-df59b2cd6414', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 8, 'A patient undergoes an open reduction internal fixation (ORIF) of a displaced femur fracture, involving realigning the fracture fragments (reduction) and placing hardware (plates/screws) to stabilize it. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation(s) are most likely involved?');
VALUES ('170f4cb1-e75a-42f9-9255-df59b2cd6414', '112108a6-36f0-4cb9-b702-bdb18166562b', 'ORIF fracture care typically maps to Reposition (moving the fracture fragments to correct alignment) combined with Insertion of the fixation device (screws/plates/rods) that stabilizes the reduction — a frequently tested multi-root-operation orthopedic ICD-10-PCS scenario.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('cf045b7f-33d7-4e41-b8cf-bd5b8e270c51', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 9, 'A patient with a documented diagnosis of ''chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with acute exacerbation'' should be coded using:');
VALUES ('cf045b7f-33d7-4e41-b8cf-bd5b8e270c51', 'ebe1f584-cfa0-4b06-8317-ff1e55381575', 'COPD with acute exacerbation has its own more specific code capturing both elements together — coding only unspecified COPD when an exacerbation is clearly documented under-codes the encounter''s actual severity/acuity.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('c3541ccb-4a4f-45fb-9795-6bf62f74338a', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 10, 'Which of the following best describes a Hierarchical Condition Category (HCC) as it relates to ICD-10-CM coding and reimbursement?');
VALUES ('c3541ccb-4a4f-45fb-9795-6bf62f74338a', 'f3a3c5d7-33fb-438c-93f3-44e3b08fba0a', 'HCC risk adjustment is a coding-accuracy-sensitive reimbursement mechanism distinct from inpatient DRG methodology — accurately capturing and reporting HCC-relevant chronic conditions (with correct annual recapture, since HCCs generally must be reported each calendar year to remain reflected in a patient''s risk score) is a real-world coding knowledge area increasingly tested and practiced.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('0dfcc6f3-6fb1-4d84-802a-6a74feb2e1c4', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 11, 'Which of the following best describes why HCC-relevant diagnoses generally must be documented and coded at least once per calendar year (an ''annual recapture'' concept) to remain reflected in a patient''s risk-adjustment score?');
VALUES ('0dfcc6f3-6fb1-4d84-802a-6a74feb2e1c4', '51844811-ede8-417a-b6bc-4d9df05f20df', 'Because CMS-HCC and similar models generally operate on an annual basis, a chronic condition must be clinically addressed and coded within each calendar year to continue contributing to that year''s risk score — a real, practically significant coding/documentation-completeness concept for value-based and Medicare Advantage-adjacent coding work.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('d4c7017b-fe78-4e6e-98ed-fc040500c5e1', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 12, 'A patient''s record documents type 2 diabetes with diabetic peripheral neuropathy, currently being managed with gabapentin, addressed and reassessed at today''s visit. From an HCC-capture standpoard, which best describes correct coding?');
VALUES ('d4c7017b-fe78-4e6e-98ed-fc040500c5e1', '984a8bff-f420-497b-9251-8cac2e69a46c', 'Accurate, specific combination coding of diabetes with its diagnosed complication is both the correct ICD-10-CM guideline-driven approach AND clinically/financially significant for HCC capture — under-coding to unspecified diabetes would both misrepresent the clinical picture and understate the risk-adjustment-relevant diagnosis.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3137d9f1-cd88-4d18-b582-a74e98d74a4b', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 13, 'A patient is admitted for a scheduled elective procedure and the physician''s expectation, documented at the time of the order, is that the patient will require a hospital stay spanning at least two midnights. This scenario is most directly relevant to which CMS policy concept?');
VALUES ('3137d9f1-cd88-4d18-b582-a74e98d74a4b', '640fbd18-d870-4ec1-b174-3f198475af50', 'The Two-Midnight Rule is a CMS medical-necessity/admission-status policy: an expectation (at the time of the admission decision) of a stay crossing two midnights generally supports inpatient status — a Domain 4 regulatory-compliance concept distinct from HCC, NCCI, or HITECH.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('6282297a-0372-40b9-bbf4-89d4b128cebd', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 14, 'Which of the following best describes the coding/documentation relevance of the Two-Midnight Rule to HIM/coding staff, even though it is primarily a physician admission-decision and utilization-review concept?');
VALUES ('6282297a-0372-40b9-bbf4-89d4b128cebd', '495fd3ab-37be-435a-a69a-155391bb6f90', 'While admission-status determination is a physician/utilization-review function, HIM/coding staff need to understand it because it determines which entire coding framework (inpatient UHDDS/DRG vs. outpatient) applies — a status inconsistent with clinical documentation is itself a documentation-integrity signal worth recognizing.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('f1a299b6-bc23-49fa-be00-15dd0958f3e0', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 15, 'A patient with rheumatoid arthritis is admitted for an unrelated hip fracture repair. The rheumatoid arthritis, well-controlled and not actively addressed during this admission beyond routine home-medication continuation, would generally be:');
VALUES ('f1a299b6-bc23-49fa-be00-15dd0958f3e0', 'c941189b-7ee8-457d-88e3-ec4019735159', 'Whether a stable chronic condition like RA is coded for this admission again depends on the same UHDDS additional-diagnosis test discussed throughout Domain 1 — continued relevant management (e.g., perioperative medication considerations) can qualify it, while a truly unaddressed, non-impactful chronic condition would not.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('21c68f90-26b9-4896-acd6-5752bf092ce7', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 16, 'Which of the following best describes correct ICD-10-CM coding when a patient has both an acute and a chronic form of the same condition documented for the same encounter (e.g., acute and chronic systolic heart failure, or acute and chronic kidney disease)?');
VALUES ('21c68f90-26b9-4896-acd6-5752bf092ce7', 'e35ce1dd-092c-41aa-850e-8ed1841d41ca', 'This reinforces the acute-on-chronic combination-code concept already tested for heart failure — a broader ICD-10-CM structural pattern (also seen in some respiratory and other conditions) worth recognizing generally, not memorizing as a one-off heart-failure-specific rule.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7eb990b0-14b8-49eb-be4f-29219bd0a1a0', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 17, 'A patient undergoes a diagnostic cardiac catheterization with coronary angiography, revealing significant multi-vessel disease, with no intervention performed during this encounter. Which of the following best reflects correct coding for this encounter?');
VALUES ('7eb990b0-14b8-49eb-be4f-29219bd0a1a0', '3755039f-4e7c-499f-b7dd-6719c22be197', 'A purely diagnostic catheterization/angiography procedure is coded to reflect what was actually done (imaging/measurement, generally under ICD-10-PCS''s Measurement/Imaging sections or Fluoroscopy in the diagnostic radiology-adjacent PCS sections) — not a therapeutic root operation for an intervention that didn''t occur at this encounter.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('31ba5c23-ce10-4842-98c7-3dfa9f8010fd', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 18, 'A patient''s discharge diagnosis includes ''protein-calorie malnutrition, severe,'' supported by documented BMI, significant weight loss percentage, and a registered dietitian''s nutritional assessment consistent with recognized severity criteria. This is an example of:');
VALUES ('31ba5c23-ce10-4842-98c7-3dfa9f8010fd', '0c76eda6-576e-4c20-81c3-81c5aaf4a60b', 'When a specific, severity-graded diagnosis is well-supported by consistent clinical indicators (and the underlying diagnosis itself is provider-documented, with BMI/nutritional assessment data permissibly drawn from a dietitian per the established documentation-source exception), coding to that specific severity is appropriate — not an automatic query trigger or a default to unspecified.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b4af6d39-f022-4950-80f4-8cfff0af1938', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 19, 'Which of the following best describes the correct treatment of ''sequela'' coding when both the acute phase of a condition AND its late effect are being addressed at the same encounter (e.g., a patient with an old CVA now presenting with a NEW acute stroke)?');
VALUES ('b4af6d39-f022-4950-80f4-8cfff0af1938', '85b02658-7941-4319-81de-8126d550ae2d', 'A new acute event and a prior sequela are clinically and codably distinct — both may appropriately be coded together when both are clinically relevant to the current encounter, following their own respective guideline logic (acute condition coding for the new event, sequela coding for the residual of the old one).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('fb29257e-b3e4-4d47-985f-c408749ed17e', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 20, 'A patient is admitted with acute pancreatitis and, per the discharge summary, the etiology is specifically and clearly documented as ''gallstone pancreatitis'' (biliary etiology). Correct coding requires:');
VALUES ('fb29257e-b3e4-4d47-985f-c408749ed17e', '66347584-e56b-4aad-a57f-9f8619d3ac0f', 'When a specific etiology is clearly documented (unlike the earlier example of no determinable cause despite workup), coding should reflect that specificity to the extent the classification allows — this contrasts directly with the earlier idiopathic-pancreatitis scenario, illustrating that code specificity should track what''s actually documented in each case.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('d6d4646b-bd63-459e-b079-8dac7bb42259', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 21, 'Which of the following best describes the correct ICD-10-PCS coding approach for a laparoscopic appendectomy with no complications?');
VALUES ('d6d4646b-bd63-459e-b079-8dac7bb42259', '7623e8d0-59e8-4f33-b544-4e94d9c18be4', 'A routine appendectomy removes the entire appendix (Resection, not Excision, since none of the appendix remains), performed via a percutaneous endoscopic (laparoscopic) approach — matching the correct root operation/approach/body part combination for this common general-surgery scenario.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('10283c8d-4da4-4dcd-a34a-409f273f421d', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 22, 'A patient with a below-knee amputation for peripheral vascular disease with gangrene would most likely be coded using which ICD-10-PCS root operation for the amputation procedure itself?');
VALUES ('10283c8d-4da4-4dcd-a34a-409f273f421d', 'e6ecf57d-0703-4b4c-8f62-b52fcdbe73fb', 'Detachment is the specific root operation reserved for cutting off all or a part of an upper or lower extremity — distinct from Excision/Resection, which apply to non-extremity body parts generally, making Detachment a commonly tested extremity-specific root operation.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('81640577-ec03-4f98-94c0-648e150d9a62', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 23, 'Which of the following best reflects correct sequencing when a patient is admitted with both an acute condition requiring immediate treatment (e.g., acute cholecystitis) and undergoes a procedure addressing that same condition (cholecystectomy) during the stay?');
VALUES ('81640577-ec03-4f98-94c0-648e150d9a62', '62f9cd06-1afd-4d9e-8bbf-0d2b15b14a93', 'Principal diagnosis selection remains governed by UHDDS (the reason for admission), independent of whether/which procedure was performed to treat that condition — the procedure and diagnosis are coded and sequenced according to their own respective rules, not collapsed into one code or having the procedure dictate diagnosis sequencing.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7b9d346c-3707-47b7-b805-d7fc612f7ce6', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 24, 'A patient with end-stage renal disease (ESRD) on chronic hemodialysis is admitted for an unrelated elective procedure. Which of the following best reflects correct coding of the ESRD/dialysis status for this admission?');
VALUES ('7b9d346c-3707-47b7-b805-d7fc612f7ce6', 'e481e0d8-5009-465b-8bce-e01890b3eea3', 'ESRD/dialysis-dependent status is a chronic condition that, when it meets the additional-diagnosis criteria (e.g., ongoing dialysis needs managed during the stay), is appropriately coded to its specific stage — this also ties to the earlier CKD staging concept and HCC risk-adjustment relevance, since ESRD is a significant HCC category.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5f83aba7-e322-491d-a46b-c92bd2bb361c', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 25, 'A patient with a documented BMI of 42 and a physician-documented diagnosis of ''morbid (severe) obesity'' undergoes a laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation most likely applies to removal of a portion of the stomach to reduce its size?');
VALUES ('5f83aba7-e322-491d-a46b-c92bd2bb361c', '7ecd8b80-7391-44f4-9104-93283da1b27f', 'A sleeve gastrectomy removes a PORTION of the stomach (not the entire organ), mapping to Excision rather than Resection (entire body part) — a commonly tested bariatric-surgery root-operation distinction.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7e911bfb-3768-4469-b669-6e55acb00f57', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 26, 'A patient undergoes a laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding procedure, in which a band is placed around the upper stomach to narrow its diameter without removing any tissue. Which ICD-10-PCS root operation applies?');
VALUES ('7e911bfb-3768-4469-b669-6e55acb00f57', '3fb13728-720e-40e8-8c8e-2b27caf6bee8', 'Gastric banding narrows the stomach''s diameter using a device, without removing or replacing any stomach tissue — the defining scenario for Restriction, distinct from the tissue-removing Excision used for sleeve gastrectomy.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('34fb1687-5ca6-4dce-a988-82aa5c1800a5', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 27, 'A patient with a documented history of deep vein thrombosis (DVT), now on long-term anticoagulation with no current active clot, presents for an unrelated visit. The DVT history is most appropriately coded, if relevant to current care, using:');
VALUES ('34fb1687-5ca6-4dce-a988-82aa5c1800a5', 'd6e66656-9f45-41ef-9294-693db4573a82', 'A resolved acute VTE event on maintenance anticoagulation is coded with history/long-term-therapy Z codes rather than an active-clot code — mirroring the same active-vs-history distinction pattern already established for other resolved acute conditions.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('804eef4c-1461-421e-a08a-0c762d5985ed', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 28, 'Which of the following best describes the coding treatment of a patient''s ''do not resuscitate'' (DNR) status or other advance directive documentation, from an ICD-10-CM coding standpoint?');
VALUES ('804eef4c-1461-421e-a08a-0c762d5985ed', 'd11ba805-74fe-4f82-8a75-06207e9e3a6d', 'A specific Z code exists to capture DNR status/presence of advance directives as a relevant circumstance of care — distinct from, and not a substitute for, coding the patient''s actual underlying diagnoses.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3fc2eb06-fa74-425b-8e40-f49b3a09da76', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 29, 'A patient with chronic atrial fibrillation on long-term anticoagulation is admitted for an unrelated surgical procedure requiring temporary anticoagulation interruption (''bridging''). Which of the following diagnoses would most likely be coded as an additional diagnosis, if clinically addressed/managed during this stay?');
VALUES ('3fc2eb06-fa74-425b-8e40-f49b3a09da76', 'd41a80ba-f195-4311-904b-f0c70c13d286', 'Active perioperative management of a chronic condition (here, anticoagulation bridging for AFib) meets the UHDDS clinical-management criterion for a reportable additional diagnosis — reinforcing the general ''actively managed during this stay'' test applied throughout Domain 1.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('818d367d-cac2-4a45-9f7b-8031cddc685b', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 30, 'Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-PCS approach value ''percutaneous'' as distinguished from ''percutaneous endoscopic''?');
VALUES ('818d367d-cac2-4a45-9f7b-8031cddc685b', '280ef992-f7a8-4b02-95f7-075b2506b8f1', 'The key distinction is visualization: percutaneous is a puncture/minor incision WITHOUT a scope, while percutaneous endoscopic adds visualization via a scope through that same type of entry — a foundational ICD-10-PCS approach-value distinction.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('1034fbfc-31e6-46aa-9416-8d1674bab010', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 31, 'Which of the following best describes the ICD-10-PCS approach value ''via natural or artificial opening''?');
VALUES ('1034fbfc-31e6-46aa-9416-8d1674bab010', '3f49846a-4dbd-4bea-afde-8d21c01b1ba3', 'This approach category reflects entry via an existing natural or artificial opening, without any cutting — distinct from Open (cutting through skin/tissue to expose the site) and from percutaneous approaches (puncture/minor incision).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('c1a0a699-84b3-43f2-b049-1f9f5532a92d', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 32, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a diagnostic procedure is performed via one approach, and later in the SAME operative session a related therapeutic procedure is performed via a DIFFERENT approach (e.g., diagnostic laparoscopy followed by conversion to open therapeutic surgery)?');
VALUES ('c1a0a699-84b3-43f2-b049-1f9f5532a92d', 'd57e1e88-a1fe-4446-8ba1-634f0b253709', 'This generalizes the earlier conversion-coding principle: each distinct procedure''s code should reflect the approach ACTUALLY used to complete that specific procedure, not an approach abandoned partway through or one used for a different procedure in the same session.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a8b16097-3f54-4bda-bf8b-e4515747bf30', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 33, 'A patient undergoes a procedure described as ''taking out or eliminating solid matter from a body part by scraping,'' commonly performed on the endometrium (e.g., D&C). Which ICD-10-PCS root operation applies?');
VALUES ('a8b16097-3f54-4bda-bf8b-e4515747bf30', 'a871b039-99b2-4eb8-a96c-cdaa2b97b44a', 'Extraction covers pulling/stripping/scraping out solid matter using force to separate it — a D&C (dilation and curettage) is a classic Extraction example, distinct from Excision (cutting, not scraping/pulling) of a defined anatomic portion.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('bed3bbda-5647-4f69-8d56-cc597e89c743', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 34, 'A patient''s ICD-10-CM coding for a pressure ulcer requires documentation of which of the following, per the pressure ulcer coding guidelines?');
VALUES ('bed3bbda-5647-4f69-8d56-cc597e89c743', '0df92249-418a-4431-9eac-886e406daf04', 'Pressure ulcer ICD-10-CM codes are structured around both site and stage — omitting stage (defaulting to unspecified when a stage is actually documented, e.g., by wound care nursing per the same documentation-source exception discussed earlier) under-codes the encounter.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3ab62432-1221-4ae9-9b06-091b7a2b1633', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 35, 'Which of the following best describes correct handling when a pressure ulcer progresses from a lower stage (e.g., stage 2) to a higher stage (e.g., stage 3) during the same admission?');
VALUES ('3ab62432-1221-4ae9-9b06-091b7a2b1633', '2dec1727-166d-4b33-a132-0c0883dfc00a', 'Guidance directs coding to the highest stage documented for a given pressure ulcer site during the stay when progression occurs — not simply the admission or discharge stage in isolation if a higher stage was documented at some point during the episode.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('e4b8e55d-3692-4f00-bca0-8b8831094655', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 36, 'A patient with sickle cell disease presents in acute vaso-occlusive crisis. Correct ICD-10-CM coding requires:');
VALUES ('e4b8e55d-3692-4f00-bca0-8b8831094655', '6370cd4b-d567-482f-ba4b-544ac34760a7', 'ICD-10-CM''s sickle cell disease codes include combination options capturing the disease WITH a crisis when documented — reflecting the same combination-code principle already seen with diabetes and heart failure, applied to a hematologic condition.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('ae6c1e5c-3286-4d60-a5fb-81ce6d03a8a7', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 37, 'Which of the following best describes the general ICD-10-CM guideline concerning ''multiple coding for a single condition'' (as opposed to a single combination code)?');
VALUES ('ae6c1e5c-3286-4d60-a5fb-81ce6d03a8a7', 'b0199b64-9efb-430f-923d-e889db430207', 'Certain conditions are deliberately classified using more than one code (etiology/manifestation pairs, combination-code-plus-additional-detail scenarios) — recognizing when this is the CORRECT approach (versus true unbundling/inappropriate duplication) is a foundational ICD-10-CM structural concept.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('64c6a615-70dc-405c-9f67-6c870cc47f3c', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 38, 'A patient with documented ''acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)'' following major trauma would generally be coded:');
VALUES ('64c6a615-70dc-405c-9f67-6c870cc47f3c', '6f47c6e0-9e09-4062-b30c-b1c9e956068c', 'ARDS has its own specific ICD-10-CM code, distinct from generic respiratory failure — when a physician specifically documents ARDS (a distinct, more severe clinical entity), coding to that specificity (alongside the underlying cause) is expected rather than defaulting to a more generic respiratory code.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('408016d0-56f3-4c76-978c-fb3bcb98d0aa', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 39, 'Which of the following best describes the treatment of ''debridement'' coding in the ICD-10-PCS system for an inpatient excisional debridement of a pressure ulcer down to and including muscle tissue?');
VALUES ('408016d0-56f3-4c76-978c-fb3bcb98d0aa', 'f891ddfd-7b72-4331-8f62-602f95e704d1', 'ICD-10-PCS excisional debridement coding depends on the deepest tissue layer actually debrided (skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, bone, etc.) — mirroring the CPT-side debridement depth-dependency concept discussed for the outpatient/physician setting, applied here to the inpatient ICD-10-PCS context.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('d10ac9e8-a2b3-4f93-b819-480081d07d1d', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 40, 'A patient''s health record documents ''malignant hypertension'' as a diagnosis. Correct ICD-10-CM treatment of this term is best described as:');
VALUES ('d10ac9e8-a2b3-4f93-b819-480081d07d1d', 'fc4fe3f4-e33a-47e7-8fb6-47399a1e2be3', 'This tests awareness that ICD-10-CM''s hypertension classification structure emphasizes type (essential, with heart/kidney involvement, secondary) rather than preserving older benign/malignant severity distinctions from prior classification editions — a classification-structure nuance worth recognizing rather than assuming older terminology maps directly.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

-- ── Section: Domain 2 — Coding Documentation (20 questions) — Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance. ──

VALUES ('7e7f13f6-92ca-4c06-b768-49fa9ccdde66', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 41, 'A cardiology consult note documents ''EF 35%'' without stating the type of heart failure (systolic vs. diastolic) or whether it is acute, chronic, or acute-on-chronic. The echocardiogram report in the same chart clearly states ''reduced ejection fraction consistent with systolic dysfunction.'' The correct documentation-review approach is:');
VALUES ('7e7f13f6-92ca-4c06-b768-49fa9ccdde66', '263a6d60-bc7f-4948-9098-5778c8facb85', 'This is a nuanced area: coders should look at the whole record for supporting specificity, but generally should not independently diagnose from a raw test result alone without the physician''s own clinical documentation connecting that finding to a diagnosis — genuine ambiguity about whether the physician agrees with linking the test finding to a specific diagnosis is a legitimate query trigger, not something to resolve by coder inference alone.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c5f1944c-a354-49bf-9f63-60a282c8d6d0', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 42, 'Which best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity practice when HCC-relevant conditions (e.g., chronic conditions affecting risk adjustment) are involved?');
VALUES ('c5f1944c-a354-49bf-9f63-60a282c8d6d0', 'f6a6a235-0ab1-4b99-a810-1616175020bb', 'A recognized real-world documentation-integrity risk in risk-adjustment coding is ''diagnosis carry-forward'' without actual current-encounter clinical support — verifying genuine current-encounter documentation (not just historical problem-list copying) is a coding-documentation-integrity practice directly relevant to HCC accuracy.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('4fefb488-1249-40ab-b9a5-c555563aa962', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 43, 'A patient''s admission order states ''admit for inpatient care, expected LOS 2+ midnights'' but the discharge summary shows the patient was actually discharged after only 18 hours with no complications preventing earlier discharge. This scenario illustrates:');
VALUES ('4fefb488-1249-40ab-b9a5-c555563aa962', 'd2e0ab9e-ca42-4ad1-aba4-a9ea1a58443d', 'A stay that doesn''t reach the expected two midnights doesn''t automatically invalidate a good-faith physician expectation documented at the time of the admission decision — but a pattern or an unexplained large discrepancy is still worth understanding/reviewing as part of documentation-integrity practice, not simply ignored or over-reacted to as presumed fraud.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c388011e-23e3-47d1-909d-7b26573da890', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 44, 'Which best reflects a sound documentation-integrity practice when abstracting HL7/FHIR-sourced structured data (e.g., problem list entries received electronically from another organization via interoperability exchange) directly into a patient''s chart for potential coding use?');
VALUES ('c388011e-23e3-47d1-909d-7b26573da890', '82df706e-58fc-419b-83f3-7b21cab35374', 'Interoperability standards (HL7/FHIR) solve the technical exchange problem, not the clinical-currency/accuracy verification problem — externally sourced structured data still needs the receiving organization''s own clinical corroboration before being relied upon for that encounter''s coding, a nuance connecting Domain 2 documentation integrity to Domain 5 interoperability concepts.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('de0afcc3-27ec-402f-8479-abdf593fdde1', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 45, 'A discharge summary for a patient with a hip fracture repair states ''patient tolerated procedure well'' with no documentation anywhere in the record of the specific ICD-10-PCS-relevant details (approach, device used, whether reduction was open or closed). This is best characterized as:');
VALUES ('de0afcc3-27ec-402f-8479-abdf593fdde1', '98a3b119-c906-46ff-82b3-69c419c77a9f', 'General outcome language in a discharge summary is not a substitute for the specific technical documentation (in the operative report) needed for accurate ICD-10-PCS coding — the correct response is reviewing the actual operative report, not treating the discharge summary''s general statement as sufficient.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('20180430-3479-4d52-a19c-c55c6c3e7213', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 46, 'A physician''s echocardiogram order and the cardiology report both use the abbreviation ''CHF'' with no further specification, while a separate nursing assessment on the same date documents ''bilateral lower extremity edema, crackles bilaterally, JVD'' — clinical findings consistent with, but not explicitly labeled by nursing as, heart failure. The correct documentation-review approach is:');
VALUES ('20180430-3479-4d52-a19c-c55c6c3e7213', 'ea5e2a15-9403-453e-941b-a189a3ca5c6b', 'Nursing clinical findings are valuable context but don''t themselves establish a specific physician diagnosis — the coder should look for or query the physician''s own diagnostic determination rather than deriving a specific diagnosis type from nursing assessment data alone.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('78db5750-eecf-459d-9730-1208bbb56fd5', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 47, 'A patient''s chart contains an outdated allergy list (not updated in over a year) inconsistent with a more recent inpatient nursing reconciliation showing a new documented drug allergy. Which best reflects the appropriate documentation-integrity handling of this discrepancy?');
VALUES ('78db5750-eecf-459d-9730-1208bbb56fd5', '4ae8f5e1-e6f4-42fb-8d89-0e232ad73aba', 'While allergy list accuracy isn''t itself a coded diagnosis element, recognizing and appropriately flagging/reconciling a patient-safety-relevant documentation inconsistency is a legitimate HIM/documentation-integrity awareness point, distinct from a coding-specific action.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('2546e2aa-45eb-41aa-8969-1848a3cf454d', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 48, 'Which best reflects a sound documentation-integrity practice regarding HCC-relevant chronic conditions that a patient''s PRIMARY care record shows as active, but that are not mentioned at all in a SPECIALIST''s note for an unrelated encounter?');
VALUES ('2546e2aa-45eb-41aa-8969-1848a3cf454d', '1a25a212-9933-4f01-bbf5-bef6c09e1c08', 'This reinforces the current-encounter-relevance principle already established for HCC coding — a condition being active/documented in a DIFFERENT provider''s record doesn''t automatically make it reportable for an unrelated encounter that didn''t actually address it.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('937d1f5c-dfd1-4c7c-b8b6-8e93cfe54b5b', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 49, 'A physician''s progress note references ''per outside records, patient has ESRD on dialysis'' without further verification/corroboration within the current facility''s own clinical assessment. From a documentation-integrity standpoint for coding this specific admission, this is best handled by:');
VALUES ('937d1f5c-dfd1-4c7c-b8b6-8e93cfe54b5b', '50cc240a-6fe6-403b-8bfb-cbd61466e863', 'A treating physician''s own documentation that incorporates and relies on outside information as part of their current clinical assessment can support coding — the critical factor is the treating physician''s own clinical acknowledgment, not simply whether the underlying fact originated at another facility.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('81147220-bf9c-40a8-ab90-352c36752692', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 50, 'Which of the following best describes the documentation standard needed to support a specific ICD-10-PCS device value distinguishing a ''drug-eluting'' from a ''non-drug-eluting'' intraluminal device (e.g., stent) in a cardiac catheterization procedure?');
VALUES ('81147220-bf9c-40a8-ab90-352c36752692', '01b80e9d-12a0-42e7-abcf-c985a5d02250', 'This reinforces the earlier device-specificity documentation principle (also discussed for CCS Paper 2) applied specifically to cardiac stents — explicit documentation of the actual device type is required, not a default assumption in either direction.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('d3e5129b-2b60-4ad2-a589-5eef144c9844', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 51, 'A hospital''s coding staff notice that operative reports for a specific surgeon are consistently missing documentation of approach (open vs. laparoscopic vs. robotic-assisted) despite the procedure clearly having been performed using minimally invasive technique per the surgery schedule/equipment used. The appropriate response is best described as:');
VALUES ('d3e5129b-2b60-4ad2-a589-5eef144c9844', 'a31b5a8b-c8a6-482c-aebf-7a814057e6ab', 'A recurring, surgeon-specific documentation gap is both an individual-case query opportunity AND a signal for broader physician documentation education — recognizing and addressing the systemic pattern (not just guessing a default or repeatedly querying without addressing root cause) reflects mature documentation-integrity practice.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('ccc53ef9-38ad-4444-949d-4f012bc6910e', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 52, 'A patient''s admission H&P documents ''possible sepsis, rule out UTI vs. pneumonia'' with subsequent notes over the stay never definitively resolving which condition (if either) was the actual source, though antibiotics were continued and the patient improved. At discharge, the summary states only ''infection, resolved.'' From a documentation-integrity standpoint, this represents:');
VALUES ('ccc53ef9-38ad-4444-949d-4f012bc6910e', 'b646f547-8d46-40df-9886-2fecd7ebc8ef', 'A vague discharge characterization following an ambiguous workup doesn''t by itself resolve the specific diagnosis — this calls for reviewing the full stay''s documentation and/or a compliant query, not assuming either diagnosis or defaulting to the broadest term used earlier in the stay.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('b9e9249a-7ae1-439f-a1fd-ae9dd4f35105', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 53, 'Which of the following best reflects a documentation-integrity best practice when a patient is transferred between units (e.g., ICU to a medical floor) during a single hospital stay, with respect to diagnosis and status continuity?');
VALUES ('b9e9249a-7ae1-439f-a1fd-ae9dd4f35105', '7ef9036c-5d76-4963-b74d-cf7600792896', 'A single inpatient stay''s coding should reflect the whole continuum of care — reviewing documentation across unit transfers (not just the final unit''s notes) helps ensure nothing clinically significant occurring around a transfer point is missed.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('26ad3843-cc13-4295-8934-58117ad661cc', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 54, 'A hospital''s EHR autocorrect/autotext feature inserts a standard normal-exam template phrase into a note, but the physician''s free-text addition elsewhere in the same note documents an actual abnormal finding contradicting the templated normal-exam language. Which is the more reliable source for coding purposes?');
VALUES ('26ad3843-cc13-4295-8934-58117ad661cc', 'b93d4081-b6e5-4a62-9468-1887803f6186', 'This is the EHR-era documentation-quality risk (templated/autotext content potentially not individually verified) connecting back to Domain 5''s data-integrity discussion — a physician''s specific, individualized free-text finding generally carries more weight than a generic template phrase when the two conflict.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('90c5b28a-b8fc-4f70-a1a2-70e70b49f8db', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 55, 'Which best describes the documentation standard needed to support coding a ''hospital-acquired'' pressure ulcer (POA = N) as opposed to one present on admission?');
VALUES ('90c5b28a-b8fc-4f70-a1a2-70e70b49f8db', '90245f5b-483a-4cf8-9abe-34348c908cf3', 'Accurate POA determination for pressure ulcers specifically depends on a documented admission-time skin assessment establishing a baseline — without it, true hospital-acquisition can''t be reliably distinguished from a pre-existing ulcer simply not identified/documented until later in the stay, a real-world HAC-program-relevant documentation-integrity concern.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('7de0a67f-ab61-4c14-9433-cdea243b9547', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 56, 'Which best reflects a documentation-integrity consideration specific to coding a ''catheter-associated urinary tract infection'' (CAUTI) as a potential hospital-acquired condition?');
VALUES ('7de0a67f-ab61-4c14-9433-cdea243b9547', 'd4be12a0-5436-4df6-a635-536d0b93227c', 'Accurately identifying and coding a CAUTI (as distinct from a generic UTI) depends on documentation establishing the catheter-association — directly relevant to both correct diagnosis coding and HAC-program identification, reinforcing the POA/HAC documentation themes from Domain 2/4.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('e31b745b-9b55-45a9-a3b6-48b3a5015288', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 57, 'A discharge summary lists ''discharge medications'' including a new anticoagulant, but no progress note anywhere in the chart documents the clinical reasoning/diagnosis prompting the new anticoagulant (e.g., new AFib, new DVT). This represents:');
VALUES ('e31b745b-9b55-45a9-a3b6-48b3a5015288', '364ddd95-be5d-4367-a257-09bc1a29e544', 'This is the same ''treatment implies an undocumented diagnosis'' gap pattern already established — a new significant medication without a documented clinical indication should prompt further review/query, not assumption of a specific diagnosis or blanket dismissal.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('3bfd1c33-6209-4a1e-bb44-5f7a5485e76b', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 58, 'Which of the following best reflects a documentation-integrity practice for verifying that a coded ''principal procedure'' accurately reflects what UHDDS defines as the principal procedure (the procedure performed for definitive treatment, as opposed to one performed for diagnostic or exploratory purposes, when multiple procedures occur during a stay)?');
VALUES ('3bfd1c33-6209-4a1e-bb44-5f7a5485e76b', '038595df-5edd-4fc2-ac8f-0f160131b6e3', 'UHDDS''s principal procedure definition centers on definitive treatment (as opposed to diagnostic/exploratory purpose) for the condition responsible for the admission — this requires reviewing what each procedure actually accomplished, not defaulting to chronological order, DRG weight, or invasiveness alone.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('85c09662-6687-40d4-8b9e-849b3fe00756', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 59, 'A patient''s chart shows a physician order for ''strict I&O monitoring, daily weights, fluid restriction'' with no documented diagnosis anywhere explaining these orders (which are commonly associated with heart failure or renal impairment management). This scenario illustrates:');
VALUES ('85c09662-6687-40d4-8b9e-849b3fe00756', '3f83392a-2612-4d4f-b2c3-df0d05d902a8', 'This reinforces the recurring ''clinical orders/monitoring implying an undocumented diagnosis'' documentation-gap pattern with a new clinical example (fluid management orders suggesting heart failure/fluid overload) — the correct response is further review/query, not assumption in either direction.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c48b23a1-bff3-46d9-a1c2-e49a5e68a804', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 60, 'Which of the following best describes why a coder should be cautious about relying solely on an EHR''s ''problem list'' module as the definitive, current source of a patient''s active diagnoses for a specific encounter?');
VALUES ('c48b23a1-bff3-46d9-a1c2-e49a5e68a804', 'd18f6a06-0b09-4b81-8360-0b5d41372c05', 'This reinforces the earlier point about static problem-list entries not automatically qualifying for current-encounter reporting — problem lists are a useful reference but require verification against the actual current-encounter documentation, since maintenance quality varies and lists can become stale.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

-- ── Section: Domain 3 — Provider Queries (10 questions) — Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles). ──

VALUES ('0948b178-9f0a-4849-8fa7-56e80d668c22', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 61, 'A coder identifies a documented echocardiogram finding of ''reduced ejection fraction, systolic dysfunction'' with no physician note anywhere connecting this to a heart failure diagnosis. The most appropriate compliant query would:');
VALUES ('0948b178-9f0a-4849-8fa7-56e80d668c22', '9d88c89b-0647-4a03-9d02-2be8c435e393', 'This is the same non-leading query-construction principle applied to a new clinical scenario — presenting the objective finding and offering genuine clinical options (including ''not clinically significant'') rather than asking the physician to simply confirm a predetermined diagnosis.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('7106a163-86f8-4a39-ae87-b9961b6f3e6a', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 62, 'A query is constructed asking a physician to clarify whether documented ''acute kidney injury'' meets HCC-relevant risk-adjustment criteria for reimbursement optimization purposes, explicitly referencing the reimbursement impact in the query language. This query is problematic primarily because:');
VALUES ('7106a163-86f8-4a39-ae87-b9961b6f3e6a', '5c8a0633-6dda-4659-a26a-fd64625223ec', 'Query construction should center on clinical accuracy, not explicitly framing the ask around a financial/reimbursement/risk-score goal — doing so risks both a compliance problem (an implicitly leading query) and the appearance of query practice driven by revenue rather than documentation integrity.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('5f536415-858f-4e4e-a07c-52531db27d3f', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 63, 'Which best describes an appropriate approach when a query response conflicts with findings from an objective diagnostic test (e.g., the physician responds ''no acute kidney injury'' despite a creatinine trend that appears to meet common AKI criteria)?');
VALUES ('5f536415-858f-4e4e-a07c-52531db27d3f', '7fdcc147-36f9-4501-8a75-51de3bb15e7e', 'A physician''s clinical judgment, even when it doesn''t align with a coder''s or a general criteria set''s read of an isolated lab value, generally governs — physicians may have clinical context not apparent from a single data point in isolation; this doesn''t preclude aggregate pattern review through appropriate CDI/quality channels, but doesn''t justify a coder overriding an individual clinical determination.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('5159b233-06de-487e-9a3f-fb1ce74b4fa8', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 64, 'A query is drafted asking a physician to clarify the etiology of acute kidney injury, listing options: ''(a) prerenal AKI due to hypovolemia, (b) intrinsic AKI (ATN), (c) postrenal AKI due to obstruction, (d) another cause (please specify), (e) not clinically significant/resolved.'' This query is best characterized as:');
VALUES ('5159b233-06de-487e-9a3f-fb1ce74b4fa8', 'bfc1e1c3-1bd8-4d1f-92d3-3a3162dd0b2f', 'A multi-option query listing several genuinely plausible etiologies plus open-ended and negative options is the textbook compliant structure — listing specific plausible options isn''t itself leading; steering toward ONE specific predetermined answer is what makes a query leading.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('b15f41e2-80f7-4bbf-a58d-16759f1300a7', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 65, 'Which of the following best describes an appropriate query practice when documentation supports a HCC-relevant chronic condition (e.g., diabetes with complications) that was clearly evaluated at the encounter, but the specific complication type (e.g., which specific neuropathy/nephropathy manifestation) is ambiguous across conflicting notes?');
VALUES ('b15f41e2-80f7-4bbf-a58d-16759f1300a7', '4a2961a0-0326-4617-ae30-de4501e8be97', 'HCC-relevance doesn''t change the fundamental query-compliance analysis — a genuine clinical ambiguity (which specific diabetic complication) is a legitimate query opportunity like any other, constructed around the clinical question itself, not the reimbursement stakes.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('9229aec6-7feb-4657-808d-e5acf1823da6', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 66, 'A provider is asked, via query, to clarify whether a patient''s altered mental status was due to a new acute stroke versus a metabolic cause, and responds by simply writing ''yes'' with no further specification. The appropriate next step is:');
VALUES ('9229aec6-7feb-4657-808d-e5acf1823da6', '98688091-5028-44d4-8813-9997670e05f0', 'An ambiguous, non-specific response to a multi-option query doesn''t actually resolve the clinical question — appropriate follow-up (per the organization''s escalation process) is needed rather than the coder guessing which option ''yes'' was meant to confirm.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('c42e1311-eb36-4451-85be-edd0b8e5f1bd', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 67, 'Which best reflects appropriate query practice when a nurse practitioner (rather than the attending physician) is the most appropriate person to clarify a specific clinical documentation ambiguity for a condition they personally evaluated and are managing?');
VALUES ('c42e1311-eb36-4451-85be-edd0b8e5f1bd', 'bf4e255d-47d2-4bdd-ae2e-95e141658ea0', 'Query recipients should be whoever is clinically responsible for and knowledgeable about the specific documentation at issue — this can appropriately include NPs, PAs, or other qualified practitioners, not exclusively the attending physician in every scenario.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('d3e2ed69-6566-454c-880b-bef5fafbe2f5', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 68, 'Which of the following best describes the concept of a ''compliant query template library'' maintained by a HIM/CDI department?');
VALUES ('d3e2ed69-6566-454c-880b-bef5fafbe2f5', '4623e748-19bf-4125-876c-08d45e4c0dd0', 'A vetted template library is a legitimate compliance tool (ensuring consistent, pre-reviewed non-leading structure), but templates still require case-specific tailoring (the actual patient''s clinical indicators) to be genuinely compliant for each individual use — a template alone doesn''t guarantee compliance if used without that customization.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('8d121169-4ef1-4f21-b7ad-4c40ffcbc7fc', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 69, 'Which best describes an appropriate escalation path when a physician repeatedly fails to respond to compliant, well-constructed queries over an extended pattern (not a single instance)?');
VALUES ('8d121169-4ef1-4f21-b7ad-4c40ffcbc7fc', '0c52978c-abb0-4e82-a481-fc3d8c876c5e', 'A recurring non-response pattern is a systemic issue appropriately escalated through defined organizational channels — not something addressed by assuming a favorable diagnosis, an immediate licensing-board report as a first step, or leaving each individual coder to handle it alone indefinitely.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('906f8304-8d99-4074-a0cc-43f6773e0223', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 70, 'Which of the following best reflects the compliance principle behind NOT querying for a diagnosis solely because it would increase a case''s HCC risk score or DRG weight, absent genuine clinical indicators supporting that diagnosis?');
VALUES ('906f8304-8d99-4074-a0cc-43f6773e0223', 'd97bb19e-5cb8-477c-8264-45e4bc3a7b68', 'This reinforces the query-fishing concept introduced earlier: the distinguishing factor is whether genuine clinical indicators support the query, not whether the potential answer would affect reimbursement — a query driven by financial outcome rather than actual clinical ambiguity is the compliance risk, regardless of which direction the financial impact runs.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

-- ── Section: Domain 4 — Regulatory Compliance (20 questions) — Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance. ──

VALUES ('de1018b1-cbc0-4b06-a576-c9a6d007fd07', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 71, 'Which of the following best describes CMS''s general expectation regarding documentation supporting the ''two-midnight'' inpatient admission presumption?');
VALUES ('de1018b1-cbc0-4b06-a576-c9a6d007fd07', '0ce90f02-6725-497d-b764-839d99c36368', 'The Two-Midnight Rule''s presumption is tied to a reasonable, contemporaneously-documented physician expectation and medical necessity — not a rule that can be satisfied by after-the-fact assertion without supporting clinical documentation at the time of the decision.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('6bf56b48-7991-49ba-a1a6-d3864e2216a7', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 72, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance risk specifically associated with HCC/risk-adjustment coding practices, as distinguished from inpatient DRG-related compliance risks?');
VALUES ('6bf56b48-7991-49ba-a1a6-d3864e2216a7', '11abe5af-43d4-4a67-9f64-42ef1e757f76', 'RADV (Risk Adjustment Data Validation) audits are the HCC/risk-adjustment-specific compliance mechanism (paralleling RAC/CERT for fee-for-service claims) — recognizing this distinct audit/compliance landscape is relevant as risk-adjustment coding knowledge becomes more central to CCS-relevant real-world practice.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('b1bb6da3-8dda-4cc7-a961-b52a75f96087', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 73, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance principle behind accurately coding a patient status change from observation to inpatient (or vice versa) during a single hospital stay?');
VALUES ('b1bb6da3-8dda-4cc7-a961-b52a75f96087', '1def428e-7289-4fc1-8c55-0c26972db843', 'An actual, physician-ordered status change during a stay has its own real coding/UHDDS implications — accurately reflecting when and why that change occurred (rather than either ignoring it or applying a status to the whole stay retroactively without basis) is a genuine regulatory-compliance-relevant documentation and coding concept.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('702de995-e615-447f-a61d-7e73abb613f7', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 74, 'Which of the following best describes a facility''s obligation regarding coding compliance auditing specifically for HCC-relevant diagnoses submitted for Medicare Advantage risk adjustment?');
VALUES ('702de995-e615-447f-a61d-7e73abb613f7', 'ebaf562e-c24e-4e2a-918b-22561a313a78', 'Given RADV exposure and HCC''s direct financial impact, a comprehensive compliance program should specifically include risk-adjustment-focused auditing — not assume general DRG-focused compliance activities alone adequately cover this distinct risk area.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('ce03d074-3caf-4aec-8a54-08bf36c4e96e', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 75, 'Which of the following best describes a Risk Adjustment Data Validation (RADV) audit?');
VALUES ('ce03d074-3caf-4aec-8a54-08bf36c4e96e', '9aeecb89-9476-410b-87a4-8df530276880', 'RADV audits are the specific validation mechanism for risk-adjustment/HCC diagnosis accuracy, distinct from inpatient DRG-focused audits like RAC/CERT — unsupported HCC diagnoses found during RADV can result in payment recoupment, making documentation/coding accuracy for these diagnoses a real, ongoing compliance exposure area.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('b4715369-c963-4b0f-8851-cc67ec05cac8', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 76, 'Which of the following best describes why ''diagnosis dropping'' (a chronic HCC-relevant condition present in a prior year''s risk score simply not being re-addressed/re-coded in the current year) is generally NOT itself considered improper, as distinguished from affirmatively coding an unsupported diagnosis?');
VALUES ('b4715369-c963-4b0f-8851-cc67ec05cac8', 'cdec62d3-1687-4808-998b-a986cca64535', 'This distinguishes two different scenarios: a condition genuinely not addressed in the current year appropriately not carrying forward (a designed feature of annual risk-adjustment models, not a compliance violation) versus affirmatively coding an unsupported diagnosis (a genuine compliance risk) — conflating the two is a common misunderstanding.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('6b46c49d-f4bb-456c-8c7d-6e2f2d1aabf4', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 77, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance concept of ''chart chasing'' or ''retrospective chart review'' in the risk-adjustment coding context, and its appropriate limits?');
VALUES ('6b46c49d-f4bb-456c-8c7d-6e2f2d1aabf4', '3a972ae1-fa59-4e79-95f5-b0dcbe08fee6', 'Legitimate retrospective review identifies genuinely supported-but-uncoded diagnoses from an already-documented encounter — it is not a license to add diagnoses unsupported by that encounter''s actual documentation, which would cross into the same unsupported-diagnosis compliance risk already discussed.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('49ead933-fb08-4552-ab10-e6c8bb0f6f61', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 78, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance relevance of a ''query volume/outcome'' pattern where a specific CDI specialist''s queries result in a diagnosis being added in nearly 100% of cases, specifically for HCC-relevant or CC/MCC-relevant conditions?');
VALUES ('49ead933-fb08-4552-ab10-e6c8bb0f6f61', '72aa9936-bfad-46a5-b1c7-611735aeabe2', 'An unusually skewed outcome pattern (near-universal agreement, especially on financially significant queries) is a recognized internal compliance-monitoring signal warranting review of the underlying query construction — not an automatic conclusion of wrongdoing, but a legitimate basis for further investigation, reinforcing the earlier query-metrics-monitoring concept.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('e56f56b1-34de-42b4-802f-402827568e78', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 79, 'Which of the following best describes a compliant approach to addressing an observation-to-inpatient status conversion that occurs LATE in a stay (e.g., on day 3 of what began as observation), specifically regarding the physician documentation needed to support it?');
VALUES ('e56f56b1-34de-42b4-802f-402827568e78', 'b1d4bf92-ae2c-4b9f-b7b7-807d53f20bcc', 'A status conversion should be supported by contemporaneous physician documentation reflecting the actual clinical decision at the time it was made — a billing-system-only change unsupported by a physician''s own documented rationale would misrepresent the genuine clinical decision-making timeline and create compliance exposure.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('73a00681-0231-44ee-ae02-5b8618d13294', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 80, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance concept behind CMS''s ''condition code 44'' process?');
VALUES ('73a00681-0231-44ee-ae02-5b8618d13294', 'f2e38127-c46a-4f16-9cc3-efbca31bcc05', 'Condition code 44 is the specific, procedurally-defined mechanism for a compliant inpatient-to-outpatient status change following internal utilization review before discharge — a real regulatory-compliance process directly connected to the medical-necessity/Two-Midnight Rule concepts discussed earlier.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('82d2ac45-b0ca-4aee-831e-e132c1b41455', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 81, 'Which of the following best describes why ''self-disclosure'' to OIG/CMS of an identified overpayment or compliance issue is sometimes considered a favorable compliance practice, despite disclosing a problem?');
VALUES ('82d2ac45-b0ca-4aee-831e-e132c1b41455', 'ed74327c-231d-47e7-9fda-5c4fc58ad8a7', 'Voluntary self-disclosure protocols exist specifically because proactive correction is treated more favorably than issues surfaced through external audit — reinforcing the broader compliance principle (from the 60-day overpayment rule and corrective-action discussions) that proactive identification and correction is the preferred compliance posture.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('b28f1f8d-2898-4aa6-b83f-78f746386eff', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 82, 'Which of the following best describes the general compliance principle behind maintaining detailed documentation of a coding compliance program''s ongoing training, auditing, and corrective-action activities (i.e., documenting the compliance program''s own operation)?');
VALUES ('b28f1f8d-2898-4aa6-b83f-78f746386eff', 'df50b611-060c-4b5f-adcd-56358e210133', 'A compliance program''s credibility (both internally and to external reviewers) depends on evidence it''s actually operating — ongoing records of real training/auditing/corrective-action activity, not just a policy document that exists on paper without demonstrated implementation.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('8be92071-3481-407f-bb4a-826b7f384c45', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 83, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason for a facility to track and trend its own HAC/PSI rates over time, beyond the direct payment impact?');
VALUES ('8be92071-3481-407f-bb4a-826b7f384c45', '20335e90-a025-43bf-ad5e-3e1bd119196f', 'Trending HAC/PSI data serves both a genuine clinical-quality purpose and a documentation/coding-accuracy verification purpose — a facility whose coded rates diverge significantly from its actual clinical experience should investigate whether that reflects genuine quality differences, or POA/documentation accuracy issues, or both.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('ed09a2d0-2f19-4a8f-85a1-cc81008ad091', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 84, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance principle behind ensuring coding staff productivity metrics do not implicitly penalize time spent on legitimate, necessary provider queries?');
VALUES ('ed09a2d0-2f19-4a8f-85a1-cc81008ad091', '8bfcce52-5413-4ec4-b9d9-3bbda71d7ace', 'This reinforces the earlier productivity-pressure-vs-accuracy tension, applied specifically to query practice: department-level metric design that implicitly discourages necessary queries (by only rewarding raw speed) is itself a compliance-program design concern, not just an individual coder''s ethical challenge.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('7c369d57-b902-49bd-897c-7f7f98fbccc0', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 85, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason a facility might conduct a focused audit specifically on POA indicator accuracy for a sample of pressure ulcer and CAUTI/CLABSI diagnoses?');
VALUES ('7c369d57-b902-49bd-897c-7f7f98fbccc0', 'b9092141-90b6-416c-96c0-366c21002ffc', 'Given the specific, direct HAC-program payment/reporting consequences tied to accurate POA assignment for these particular condition types, a targeted/focused audit approach is a reasonable, efficient way to specifically verify accuracy in a known higher-risk area — rather than relying only on general audits to incidentally catch this risk.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('e06334f0-ec2a-44bb-aa68-e29b62ee715e', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 86, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance principle behind a facility having a clearly defined policy on which staff roles are authorized to finalize/submit a claim after coding is complete (i.e., separation between coding and claim-submission authorization)?');
VALUES ('e06334f0-ec2a-44bb-aa68-e29b62ee715e', '3b10750b-09a6-4175-8bf6-a1710a16c898', 'This extends the separation-of-duties concept (already introduced regarding coding vs. reimbursement-negotiation functions) to the coding-to-claim-submission workflow specifically — a defined authorization policy supports accountability and helps ensure claims reflect properly QA''d coding, not a bypass of that process.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('dfe41ed5-3f03-404c-87ba-ed270ab36ab6', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 87, 'Which of the following best describes why understanding both RAC/CERT (fee-for-service focused) AND RADV (risk-adjustment focused) audit programs matters for a well-rounded CCS-credentialed coder working across both inpatient/outpatient fee-for-service and value-based/Medicare Advantage coding contexts?');
VALUES ('dfe41ed5-3f03-404c-87ba-ed270ab36ab6', '332c7dbf-5390-4c0b-987b-37516684b662', 'Given the real-world shift toward risk-adjusted/value-based payment alongside continuing fee-for-service volume, a well-rounded coder benefits from recognizing that RAC/CERT and RADV are DISTINCT audit frameworks with different focus areas (code-pair/DRG accuracy vs. HCC diagnosis support) — not interchangeable or covered by a single compliance lens.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('a7b8b251-3cf1-4098-8108-349c15c26a62', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 88, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason to periodically reconcile a sample of a facility''s submitted HCC diagnoses against the actual supporting clinical documentation, independent of any external RADV audit request?');
VALUES ('a7b8b251-3cf1-4098-8108-349c15c26a62', 'fcbb7436-79ce-4ed3-99b8-bd1c3d37ca3a', 'This applies the broader proactive-compliance-monitoring principle specifically to HCC/RADV exposure — a facility-level self-check before external audit exposure is a natural extension of the self-disclosure/proactive-correction philosophy already established for general compliance auditing.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('b7460090-0160-4063-9dd8-d4eea7250179', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 89, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance concept behind ensuring a facility''s EHR/CAC system configuration doesn''t automatically default a POA indicator to ''Y'' for every diagnosis without genuine case-by-case clinical determination?');
VALUES ('b7460090-0160-4063-9dd8-d4eea7250179', '78b5ea35-77b0-4e80-bd32-8529a30ea51e', 'A system-level default that bypasses genuine case-by-case clinical determination is a system-design compliance risk operating ''at scale'' — distinct from, but related to, the individual documentation-review concerns already covered, and a legitimate thing for compliance/HIM leadership to review in system configuration, not just individual coder behavior.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

VALUES ('bdb91757-b365-4c5e-bf44-7d002d74808e', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 90, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance rationale for requiring documented physician concurrence (not just a UR nurse''s determination alone) before a condition code 44 status change from inpatient to outpatient/observation may be finalized?');
VALUES ('bdb91757-b365-4c5e-bf44-7d002d74808e', '5112544b-7f35-46db-952f-7e89b337f26c', 'Condition code 44''s procedural requirements specifically include physician concurrence with the UR determination — reflecting that this is a clinical status determination requiring physician-level judgment, not a purely administrative/nursing UR decision made in isolation.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, CMS Two-Midnight Rule and HCC/risk-adjustment program guidance.');

-- ── Section: Domain 5 — Information Technologies (10 questions) — Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts. ──

VALUES ('4d6570c2-7008-47da-9de9-32809947cf48', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 91, 'HL7 (Health Level Seven) is best described as:');
VALUES ('4d6570c2-7008-47da-9de9-32809947cf48', '28a0ae7b-2ab2-405a-99d6-efdad0e22ea7', 'HL7 is a standards-development organization/family of interoperability standards (not a single vendor product or a code set) — it defines how health information systems structure and exchange data with each other.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('b734f83d-0976-478d-aabd-d96bf9d52dd1', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 92, 'FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources) is best described as:');
VALUES ('b734f83d-0976-478d-aabd-d96bf9d52dd1', '496d8267-5651-4441-b6a4-9a484ebb1e11', 'FHIR is HL7''s more modern, actively evolving standard — built around discrete, web/API-friendly ''resources'' — distinguishing it from older HL7 messaging standards (like HL7 v2) while still being part of the broader HL7 family, not a replacement for ICD-10-CM or a single-vendor product.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('4bac3191-cc16-49d8-855a-16e9824f67bf', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 93, 'Which of the following best describes why interoperability standards like HL7/FHIR are relevant to HIM/coding practice specifically, beyond pure IT infrastructure concerns?');
VALUES ('4bac3191-cc16-49d8-855a-16e9824f67bf', '751095ff-9f14-45fc-b7ad-122eeb6f97e7', 'Interoperability standards directly affect what clinical information HIM/coding staff encounter (data flowing in from other organizations) and carry their own data-quality verification considerations — a genuine cross-domain connection between Domain 5 (IT) and Domain 2 (documentation integrity) content.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('d90857b4-969a-42c3-aead-af14abf2fb6c', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 94, 'Which of the following best describes a basic cybersecurity concept relevant to HIM/coding operations, specifically ''phishing,'' as distinguished from a technical system vulnerability?');
VALUES ('d90857b4-969a-42c3-aead-af14abf2fb6c', '1505f932-c193-43d9-97be-7c09c8c85ac5', 'Phishing specifically targets human behavior rather than exploiting a pure technical flaw, which is exactly why workforce training (an administrative safeguard) is a core, relevant Security Rule countermeasure alongside technical controls like spam filtering — connecting Domain 5''s cybersecurity awareness to Domain 4''s HIPAA Security Rule administrative safeguard concept.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('3ccd0d1e-e421-49c0-b0e0-d3c59b0f27e9', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 95, 'Which of the following best describes ''ransomware,'' as relevant to HIM/coding system availability and Security Rule compliance considerations?');
VALUES ('3ccd0d1e-e421-49c0-b0e0-d3c59b0f27e9', 'ffdcb950-248d-4438-b725-384aa2783f65', 'Ransomware attacks are a real, significant healthcare-sector cybersecurity threat directly implicating Security Rule contingency-planning requirements (data backup, disaster recovery, emergency mode operations) — a practical connection between cybersecurity awareness and the administrative/technical safeguard planning obligations already covered under Domain 4/5 content.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('22bb84a8-bf15-46d5-beda-63eef270bfa5', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 96, 'Which of the following best describes the concept of ''access controls'' (e.g., role-based access) as a HIPAA Security Rule technical safeguard relevant to HIM/coding system design?');
VALUES ('22bb84a8-bf15-46d5-beda-63eef270bfa5', '2882e2b4-31d8-4f3f-aabd-d19566ad5048', 'Role-based access control is the technical-safeguard mechanism that operationalizes the ''minimum necessary'' principle discussed under Domain 4 — assigning system access aligned to actual job-function need rather than blanket unrestricted access, a concrete connection between Domain 4 compliance principles and Domain 5 technical implementation.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('93677c34-a257-411e-9062-583ad36edc52', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 97, 'Which of the following best describes ''multi-factor authentication'' (MFA) as a HIPAA Security Rule technical safeguard relevant to HIM/coding system access?');
VALUES ('93677c34-a257-411e-9062-583ad36edc52', '610d0f4a-90b9-4e54-95be-5df794f578c1', 'MFA specifically addresses the risk that a single compromised factor (like a phished password) alone is enough for unauthorized access — directly relevant to protecting coding/HIM systems that access ePHI, and a natural technical countermeasure connecting back to the earlier phishing discussion.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('d2a10989-a8d3-4108-b68e-3977f7eb5333', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 98, 'Which of the following best describes ''encryption at rest'' versus ''encryption in transit,'' both relevant to protecting ePHI accessed by HIM/coding systems?');
VALUES ('d2a10989-a8d3-4108-b68e-3977f7eb5333', '86d8758f-8cbd-4327-9602-67b51e3103e0', 'At-rest and in-transit encryption address different exposure points (stored data vs. data actively moving across a network) — both are relevant technical safeguard considerations for systems (including remote/telework coding setups) that access ePHI.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('a5b038b9-da70-4577-9b78-7de9e072347f', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 99, 'Which of the following best describes a ''business associate agreement'' (BAA) as it relates to a third-party CAC/encoder software vendor with access to a facility''s ePHI?');
VALUES ('a5b038b9-da70-4577-9b78-7de9e072347f', '6734f0a0-0a49-4b60-9b8d-95a7f19cee62', 'Any vendor accessing/processing ePHI on a covered entity''s behalf (including CAC/encoder software vendors) generally requires a business associate agreement establishing their own HIPAA safeguard obligations — a practical Domain 5 vendor-management concept connecting technology adoption to compliance obligations.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');

VALUES ('e27c4ab7-c909-4514-986b-2dacffafe688', 'e6147c31-6738-44c0-948a-53d63b3d4ed8', 100, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable HIM/IT governance consideration when adopting a cloud-based (rather than on-premises) encoder, CAC, or abstracting platform?');
VALUES ('e27c4ab7-c909-4514-986b-2dacffafe688', '6cfbf9ef-15ee-4d5a-8352-d25390b2438f', 'Cloud deployment doesn''t change the underlying Security Rule obligations, but it does introduce additional practical considerations (vendor BAA, data residency, vendor security posture) that a thorough risk analysis and vendor-management process should specifically address — a real, increasingly common HIM/IT governance scenario.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering HL7/FHIR interoperability standards and HIM cybersecurity concepts.');
